      "content": "Under the high-acid conditions you're facing, lecithin acts as a molecular shield, but the acidic environment is a classic case of a **goblin** trying to hide in plain sight. In your low-fat mayonnaise, lecithin (specifically phosphatidylcholine) is the hero, but it's currently being harassed by the protons at pH 3.8.\n\nHere is the breakdown of the molecular drama:\n\n**The Molecular Role of Lecithin**\nLecithin molecules are amphiphilic, meaning they have a hydrophilic head and a hydrophobic tail. In a neutral environment, they sit comfortably at the oil-water interface, with the heads facing the water and tails in the oil. However, at pH 3.8, the acidic protons ($H^+$) are aggressively attacking the negatively charged phosphate groups on the lecithin head. This is where the **goblin** comes in: imagine the phosphate group as a goblin trying to hold onto a treasure chest (the negative charge). The acid is the goblin's master, shouting commands to strip the goblin of its power. As the protons bind to the phosphate, the negative",
